**Quick answer:** A useful recruiting dashboard answers "what needs me today?" before "how are we doing?" Each morning, check today's interviews, overdue interview feedback, finished screening interviews, candidate replies, jobs waiting on approval or about to close, and applicants nobody has looked at. The GetHired company dashboard ranks these in one **Needs attention** list, with the next action on each row.

If hiring isn't your full-time job, it competes with everything else. Candidates feel the delay. The fix is a short, regular check that starts with whatever is blocked on you.

## What should a recruiting dashboard show first?

Actions, then trends. A chart of last month's applications is useful to review, but it won't tell you that a strong candidate has been waiting a week.

On GetHired, the line at the top summarises what's waiting, such as interviews today and applicants waiting. If there's nothing, it says you're all caught up.

## What's in the Needs attention list?

Rows are ranked in this order, with the longest waiting first within each kind. Closing jobs are ordered by soonest date.

{% table %}
-
- Item
- Why it's there
- What to do
---
- Overdue interview feedback
- A scorecard is late
- Submit yours, or remind the panel
---
- AI screening interview to review
- A candidate finished and is waiting
- Read the report
---
- Interested reply to outreach
- A candidate said yes to a conversation
- Open it and follow up
---
- Job waiting for approval
- A role can't go live yet
- Review it
---
- Job closing soon
- It closes within 3 days
- Extend it if you need more applicants
---
- Applicants waiting in Applied
- Nobody has moved them
- Review, and move them on
---
- Unfinished draft
- A job you started hasn't been published
- Finish it
{% /table %}

Each thing appears once, and the list shows the top 8 with links to the rest. Where your role allows, some rows let you act right there, such as reminding a panel or moving an applicant to another stage.

## Why does everyone see a different dashboard?

The dashboard follows each person's [role and permissions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/hiring-as-a-team-custom-roles-permissions-and-hiring-teams-on-gethired). Someone assigned to two jobs sees applicants and funnel counts for the jobs they're allowed to view, not the whole company's. Sections you don't have access to aren't shown.

So a hiring manager can open the same page as a founder and see a list that's relevant to them.

## What does a 10-minute morning hiring routine look like?

1. **Minutes 1–2: today's interviews.** Open the kit and reread the resume.
2. **Minutes 2–4: your overdue feedback.** Write it before you read anyone else's. See [independent interview scorecards](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/interview-scorecards-how-to-collect-independent-feedback-from-your-panel).
3. **Minutes 4–6: replies and finished screening interviews.** These people have just done something and are waiting.
4. **Minutes 6–9: applicants in Applied.** Start with the strongest fit, then work through the longest waiting.
5. **Minute 10: jobs.** Approve, extend or pause as needed.

If you can't finish, stop after the people who are waiting on you. Drafts can wait.

## What should you review weekly instead?

Trends need time to mean anything, so look at them once a week.

{% table %}
-
- Metric
- Question it answers
---
- Applicants per stage
- Where is the pipeline thin or crowded?
---
- Conversion between stages
- Where do we lose people?
---
- Days in each stage
- Where are we slow?
---
- Source of candidates
- Where do applicants come from?
---
- Time to hire
- How long does the whole process take?
{% /table %}

On GetHired, the dashboard's **Pipeline** card shows applicants per stage and how many arrived in the last 7 days. The funnel on each job's applications page adds conversion from the previous step and median days in each stage. With only a few applicants, treat percentages as hints, not conclusions. Our [recruiting metrics guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/recruiting-metrics-for-startups-time-to-hire-funnel-conversion-and-source-of-hire) explains each metric.

## What should a new company do first?

A new account also gets a setup checklist, limited to the steps your role can take:

- add your logo and company description;
- post your first job;
- [publish your careers page](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/build-a-branded-careers-page-and-custom-application-form-in-minutes);
- invite a teammate;
- [bring in candidates you already have](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/candidate-rediscovery-how-to-turn-old-resumes-and-spreadsheets-into-a-searchable-talent-pool).

## Can you ask questions instead of reading the dashboard?

Yes. The dashboard suggests prompts for the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat), and you can ask your own, such as "Who applied this week and who should I talk to first?" Actions that email candidates or move applications wait for your approval.

## Frequently asked questions

### What is a recruiting dashboard?

A page that summarises your hiring: what needs action now, and how candidates are moving through your stages.

### How often should a small team check its hiring dashboard?

Once a day while you have open roles, and a longer review of trends once a week.

### Which hiring metric matters most?

It depends on your problem. If candidates drop out, look at days in each stage. If too few are suitable, look at sources and your job description.

### Can I see the dashboard if I only interview?

You see what your role allows. Interviewers who have no company-wide access still get the kit and scorecard for interviews they're on.

